  "answer": "Phototropism is the movement of plants in response to light. Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin conducted experiments on phototropism using the coleoptile (the tip of the stem of a seedling). They found that when the tip of the coleoptile was covered with a metal cylinder, the seedling did not bend towards the light. However, when light was allowed to pass through the cylinder, the seedling bent normally. This suggested that the tip of the coleoptile was responsible for sensing the light and transmitting an 'influence' to the lower part of the plant, causing it to bend. Later, in 1926, F.W. Went discovered that this 'influence' was a chemical substance called auxin, which is a plant hormone. Auxin is produced in the tip of the coleoptile and moves to the lower part of the plant, causing the plant to bend towards the light. This process is known as phototropism.",
